so  hello everyone thanks for attending this  session my name is pedro zabe and today  i will be talking about the solution  developed by perkin helmet for coveted  sequencing  the next flex variants are scovichoked  so the emergence of mutants as copied to  variance  associated with changes to transmission  and health outcomes  have shown that tracking and  identification of wipes variants is  critical to establish an effective  public health response  as a consequence of this once a sample  is detected as positive for kobe 19  using an artificial assay labs are  more and more encouraged to find whether  this particular sample contains  a variant of concern or not to do so  three steps are necessary library  preparation  where the sample is made ready for  sequencing the sequencing itself where  the data is generated  and finally data analysis we will review  in the next slides how each of these  steps are handled by our solution in  detail so let's focus first on the  library equation  the next flex body and sick has been  designed to make  covet sequencing easy the kit is  available in 96  and 304 reaction format and it's an  all-inclusive solution  meaning it is that all the components  that you might need to prepare samples  for sequencing are there  the regions the barcodes and the  magnetic bits  also software is part of the product we  will talk more about this later  so now let's go a little bit into the  details of the workflow  as you would imagine the sample so the  input for the next flex variant 6  has copied to kit is extracted genetic  material from the swap  or saliva center was used for visuality  pcr assay  so we take this extracted material and  then first of all  the workflow will convert the rna  containing it  into dna this is a standard in ngs  workflows for rna afterwards  the viral dna will be amplified using  the articular primers that cover the  whole genome of saskov2  and have been thoroughly tested by the  community finally those amplicons will  be processed using a standard library  prep  this workflow has a total turnaround  time of about 7 hours  as we just mentioned the input material  is the same extract used  for rtq pcr assay and there is no need  to do offers extraction or any illusion  no manipulation of any kind is required  samples with different biology can be  used and we know that this could have no  impact on body identification  performance  in fact we've been validating our kids  with real clinical samples and we know  that  most clinical samples that test positive  will have a viral load that is  compatible so it's within the range of  our source  so copic sequencing is an application  where many samples are processed  in this case automation is relevant uh  and then  we thought of just showing a quick  view of the automation solutions we  offer  just remember at parking armor we have  our own automated liquid handlers for  ngs  and we have plenty of experience in this  field our nexus variancy kit  can be used manually and also can be  automated  using our instruments such as cyclone g3  and gs  cyclone iq and c5 so we have all the  scripts readily available  and then different proposals for  automated workflow  depending on the throughput of the  laboratory so we have solutions or  proposals for 96 samples per day  up to more than 700 per day  don't forget that in addition to the  instruments we can provide also the  consumables for automation so basically  working with perkinemer you can have a  single supplier for all your needs for  copied sequencing  now let's review briefly the part of the  process corresponding to the sequencing  first of all we should say that our  solution is compatible with any illumina  sequencer  from an isaac to an obesity  so this our kit is quite flexible and  different sequencing settings are  possible  however we recommend single end  sequencing with a read length of 36  nucleotides basically to have a quick  turnaround time in all cases so  regardless of the sequencing settings  that you use  a minimum of 1 million clusters per  sample  is recommended so just to illustrate the  relevance of the sequencing settings  in time of sequencing please have a look  in the  to the plot on the top of the slide to  see quantitative qualitatively how  sequencing  time is impacted by doing parent or  or by increasing the real length for  example  on the table visual you can see that an  exit instrument  using our recommended settings you can  obtain data  in less than 11 hours whereas most  solutions in the market  are recommending 2x150 which has a  sequencing time of about 29 hours so for  an application where quick response time  is crucial  this is a factor quite important factor  to have in mind  finally in the table presented in this  slide  you can see the clusters generated at  each instrument  these numbers are equivalent to the  maximum number of samples that you can  load per run  if you follow our recommendation of one  million clusters per sample as we  discussed  previously so  in an isik you you can sequence up to  four samples  nexiq which is probably one of the most  used instruments for copied application  you can sequence between 130 and 400  samples  and then with nova 6 due to the high  multiplexing  capabilities of our barcodes then you  can sequence  more than 10 000 samples per run using  an s4 frozen  so now let's talk briefly about the  analysis  part of the process so customers using  the next less variant six has covered  two kit have access to free data  analysis  the solution we offer is a web-based  application  not requiring any installation and it's  really usage really easy to use  just in two steps customers will be able  to drop their first two files so they  raw data and their analysis will start  automatically nothing else is needed  solar performs a number of steps and we  wanted just to  go over them for you to have an  understanding of what is doing  on the first place data  is mapped against the wuhan reference  strain  globally used as reference for salt kobe  2  then accuracy is applied to the data so  to ensure that at least 90 out of 98  amplicons generated by the arctic  primers are fully covered  this is crucial to ensure that we are  not missing an imitation  then the actual mutations are called and  finally  the variant is a sign  in this slide you can see an example of  a real report  generated by the server this is a real  clinical sample as i was telling  so the most important part of the report  is the summary  under this section you will have  the main results of the test you will  have sequencing metrics such as the  percentage of base  cover and then also you can see the  pangolin lineage which corresponds to  the variant that we want to determine  if the variant is considered a spinal  concept  this lineage will be flagged  as craft color after this summary you  have an additional table with all the  changes detected in the sample with  respect to the reference genome  it wasn't familiar with this application  so pangolin is the standard tool used  for saskov2 linux classification and  nomenclature  and here you have in the slide a link to  the website so you can visit  then you will find that in addition to  be a tool for classifying  your data it also contains a lot of  information on major lineages and  epidemiology that is publicly available  i wanted to mention that in this report  in addition to the pangolin language  information  there is resource information based on  next class classification  this is a different open source  algorithm for saskatchewan  classification that is also widely used  as i was telling it's also included in  the report  and then this is all that i wanted to  discuss with you today  just a brief introduction to our our  solution for copic sequencing  thanks for your attention and i'll be  happy to take any questions you may have

